WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.810
There's a really good article making the rounds
about supposedly independent swing vote

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:11.250
and the argument is from this article that
essentially they don't really exist. And this

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.630
is something I've been talking about with
you guys for years at this point. As you know,

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:20.189
if you've been around for a while with the
show, I have frequently questioned the reality

00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:26.269
of a truly independent or undecided voter
this election, especially when we think how

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:32.070
could someone really be undecided between
Donald Trump and really just about anything

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:36.760
else. But even beyond this election or beyond
the world of Trump. This has been a question.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.690
In this particular case, the contrast are
so clear between Trump and anything else that

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:48.140
anybody who's paying any attention and who
understands what's going on, it seems almost

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:54.380
inconceivable that there would really be an
independent swing voter that would rationally

00:00:54.380 --> 00:01:00.820
and carefully decide either to vote for Donald
Trump or for somebody else. And this idea

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:06.460
of an independent swing voter has actually
almost turned into a meme, the meme of the

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:11.340
enlightened centrist because it makes such
little sense quite frankly.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.890
And we saw some interviews on CNN a few months
ago, which I played for you with uneducated

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:20.170
white women. What I mean by that is is white
women without college educations, some of

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:24.990
whom had voted for Barack Obama and later
for Donald Trump. And it was a combination

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:30.530
as we saw of people who were simply uninformed
about the positions of the candidates and

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:36.380
were sort of stumbling around. And then also
some simple ignorance was a factor as well.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:40.729
And this gets us to political scientists,
Rachel byte coffers theory, which is there

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:46.299
is no such thing as an actual independent
swing voter. Rachel byte coffer very accurately

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:51.420
predicted the results of the 2018 midterms.
And one of the things that her model does

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:57.530
is it doesn't care about so-called independent
swing voters. And in fact her theory about

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:02.200
elections lines up perfectly with something
that I've been screaming about to anybody

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.549
who will listen since the day Donald Trump
was elected.

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:11.210
Elections are not shaped by people who changed
their minds, but they are changed rather by

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:17.200
who decides to vote. I've been saying for
years now, trying to convince Trumpists to

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:24.000
vote for a Democrat is not a strategy and
it is a waste of time. Finding people on our

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.490
side and getting them to vote is the way to
go. There's this conventional wisdom which

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:37.700
is not always so wise that there's something
like a, if you have 55 turnout in the U S

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:44.320
which is a common turnout for elections, that
15% of that 55% that chooses to vote are so-called

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:50.160
swing voters and they turn the election. You
have to convince those swing voters to vote

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:58.260
for your candidate and this assumes way more
rationality, effort and consideration than

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.920
most voters are actually putting into voting
decisions. It's not a pejorative ad hominem,

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:08.110
it's just the reality. And to quote Rachel
byte coffer, she says the idea that there

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:14.290
is this informed and engaged American population
that is watching these political events and

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:19.040
watching their elected leaders and assessing
their behavior and making a judgment and it

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.090
is just not true.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:26.950
And as she points out, it's not really about
candidates swinging individual voters from

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:32.910
one candidate to another. It's about candidates
engaging and activating certain coalitions

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:38.020
and groups of voters. Now, when other political
scientists are asked about Rachel's theory,

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:42.950
many disagree and they say, no way, there
are swing voters. There's tons of people who

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:50.260
voted for Obama and then ultimately for Trump
in 2016 or voted for Bernie in the primary

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:55.720
and then Trump or Hillary in the 2008 primary
and all these different contingencies of people

00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:01.180
that appear to swing from one side to the
other. They exist of course anecdotally and

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:06.830
in small numbers, but that really what you
are talking about is not a number that swings

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:12.660
elections. What changes in a more significant
way is who is engaged and therefore who shows

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:18.360
up. It's not the same people switching between
Republican and democratic candidates.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:24.710
That is moving elections. Now, if you believe
Rachel byte coffer, this suggests a totally

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:30.860
different election strategy and different
metrics for evaluating who can win the electability

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:35.850
question. When you look at a candidate, if
Rachel is correct, you think about would an

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:42.530
imaginary regular voter choose Trump or this
candidate? That's the wrong way to think about

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:49.660
it. Instead, according to Rachel's theory,
you would say, what effect would this candidate

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:55.390
have on who chooses to come out and vote for
them or against them or their opponent for

00:04:55.390 --> 00:05:01.970
their opponent and who chooses simply to stay
home? Would Bernie get more Trumpists to vote

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:07.210
for Donald Trump? That's the type of question
you ask. If you believe this model instead

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:12.720
of the swing voter model, and of course if
you believe this model, the campaign strategy

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:18.240
shifts away from arguing with people you don't
agree with to try to convince them to vote

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:25.320
for your candidate simply to finding people
that are already basically on your side and

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:29.770
making sure that they understand the importance
of getting out to vote and activating voters,

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:34.270
which is exactly what I've been saying for
year, for years, and this is very obvious

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:36.530
in a very superficial sense.

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:42.000
If you find 10 people that generally agree
with your political view who didn't vote last

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.450
time, the amount of time you would have to
spend to get a couple of them to go out and

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:54.580
vote is going to be far less than finding
one Trumpist and arguing with them about why

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:59.060
they should vote for somebody other than Trump.
In fact, presenting the Trumpist with facts

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:03.300
about why their view that Trump is the right
person is completely wrong. As we know from

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:09.040
behavioral economics is almost guaranteed
to fail and very often gets people more sort

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:13.260
of ingrained in their preexisting beliefs.
If you find people that already agree with

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:18.370
you and make it easy for them to vote, offer
to help them to vote. Give them some general

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:23.260
ideas about why it would be really important
that they go out there and vote. You're not

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:26.750
really convincing them of much other than
saying you're allowed to vote and you should

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:27.750
vote in.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:34.030
That's it. You will make way more progress
that way and that correlates so tightly with

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:38.610
the theory that Rachel bike coffer is putting
forward here. Let me know what you think about

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:44.590
her article, which is or the story about her
rather in Politico which has spread wide,

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:49.330
uh, S been spread very widely over the weekend.
Let me know what you think also about how

00:06:49.330 --> 00:07:59.110
this relates to contingencies and different
enclaves of voters

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:07.860
in the 2020 election. Specifically,

